{
  "title": "Discussion",
  "content": [
    {
      "id": "text",
      "text": "The dominance of hybrid CNN-LSTM models reflects a broader trend: combining spatial and temporal learners yields complementary benefits. However, Transformer-based architectures (GCTrafo) show emerging superiority, particularly for multi-site forecasting.\n\nA critical limitation is geographic overfitting — models validated on one location may fail in different climate zones (tropical, arid, temperate). Only one included study performed cross-location validation. Transfer learning and domain adaptation remain underexplored.\n\nUncertainty quantification is another gap. Deterministic point forecasts dominate; probabilistic forecasts (quantile regression, Bayesian neural networks, conformal prediction) appear in fewer than 15% of studies. Grid operators increasingly demand prediction intervals, not just point estimates."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "title": "Conclusion",
  "content": [
    {
      "id": "text",
      "text": "This SLR synthesized 18 high-quality studies on ML/DL-based solar PV power forecasting (2021–2025). Hybrid CNN-LSTM architectures dominate and achieve strong performance (R² > 0.90), but Transformer-based models are gaining ground. Key gaps include limited geographic generalization, insufficient uncertainty quantification, and lack of standardized benchmarks. We recommend: (1) developing open benchmark datasets across climate zones, (2) prioritizing probabilistic forecasting, and (3) advancing transfer learning methods for cross-location generalization."
    }
  ]
}
